1ss3|n|An instrument with which anything is scraped. ; An instrument by which the soles of shoes are cleaned from mud and the like, by drawing them across it. ; An instrument drawn by oxen or horses, used for scraping up earth in making or repairing roads, digging cellars, canals etc. ; An instrument having two or three sharp sides or edges, for cleaning the planks, masts, or decks of a ship. ; In the printing press, a board, or blade, the edge of which is made to rub over the tympan sheet and thus produce the impression. ; One who scrapes. ; One who plays awkwardly on a violin. ; One who acquires avariciously and saves penuriously.	a:1jda,79,4;197b,55,1;28ax,50,4~1jda,79,4;28ax,50,4;197b,55,1	n:1fsp,98,9;197b,71,3;ber,50,2;28ax,46,3~1fsp,98,9;28ax,46,3;197b,71,3;ber,50,2	N:804,78,3~804,78,3
1ss4|n|of Scrape ; The act of scraping; the act or process of making even, or reducing to the proper form, by means of a scraper. ; Something scraped off; that which is separated from a substance, or is collected by scraping; as, the scraping of the street.	a:1d2v,43,1;28ax,11,1~1d2v,43,1;28ax,11,1	N:1cp0,37,2;1w9r,18,2;1lwk,5,1~1cp0,37,2;1w9r,18,2;1lwk,5,1
